  "observations": [
    "v1/v2 use two-argument native peer setup and incorrectly expect a generic0x4000 reply; v2 records native type0 context response instead. These are harness-protocol failures, not capacity refusals",
    "v3 switches to standard one-argument client setup with12-byte type array; requester3 and7..14 all receive0x4000 setup and initial menu offers",
    "v4 corrects root-menu magic0xffffff and render total to actual offered count; nine open sessions accept menus. Requester3 root renders one item type129; requester7 track render times out",
    "v5 reduces to requesters3 and7: requester3 root7/render129 succeeds, requester7 track2547 offer succeeds but render times out",
    "The control does not replace local reserved requesters5/6 or serve hub libraries through native USB slots",
    "Native process remains967182/EEEE with exact published0.3.2 ELF after controls",
    "v6 matched0x1004 queries and64-row render pages with actual total2547: requester3 returns64 items, requester7 times out; source/current command/page-size differences are removed, membership/routing still unproven"
  ],
  "interpretation": "A successful setup/menu offer does not prove usable extra-requester sessions. Do not implement sharing by assuming seven-through-fourteen requester IDs can render or safely replace existing local/native communicators. Native session/export isolation remains a design gate.",
  "limits": [
    "Not a universal native capacity proof; requester7 overlaps a private member number and wire-state details may matter",
